<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hindu Kush</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> Kush is a mountain range in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> that separates <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a>. Its passes were the routes used by invaders entering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> from the north-west. Rulers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khanate">Chagatai Khanate</a>, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duwa">Duwa</a>, raided across it into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>'s territory.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hindu-kush.jpg" alt="Hindu Kush" width="320" height="178" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ninara from Helsinki, Finland · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hindu_Kush_mountains_(30357238428).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The range is about 800 kilometres long and lies west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himalayas">Himalayas</a>, running from central and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> into north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> and far south-eastern Tajikistan. It divides the valley of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Amu Darya</a>, the ancient Oxus, in the north from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> valley in the south. Its highest point is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirich-mir">Tirich Mir</a>, at 7,708 metres in Pakistan. Numerous high passes, known as kotal, make a network for caravans, and the Salang Pass north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> is the most important in Afghanistan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The name Hindu Kush is first mentioned by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> in the 14th century, who explained it as Hindu-slayer, because slaves taken from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> to Turkestan died in the harsh climate. Other scholars take it as a corruption of Hindu Koh, mountains of India. In the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> it was the Caucasus Indicus. Its significance was recorded from the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>, and Alexander's army crossed it in the spring of 329 BCE.</p><p>After Alexander's death in 323 BCE the region passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a>, then around 305 BCE to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire</a>, and around the start of the common era to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushan-empire">Kushan Empire</a>. Buddhism flourished there, with monasteries, trade networks and the rock-carved Bamiyan Buddhas. Kabul fell to the army of the Abbasid caliph <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mamun">al-Ma'mun</a> in 808. The Hindu Shahi dynasty of Kabul later held the range until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabuktigin">Sabuktigin</a> conquered its lands west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshawar">Peshawar</a>. The people of Kafiristan kept polytheistic traditions until 1896, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghans">Afghans</a> under Amir Abdur Rahman Khan converted them, and the region was renamed Nuristan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The mountains have long supplied lapis lazuli, mined in the Kowkcheh Valley, and emeralds from the Panjsher valley. Mountain areas are mostly barren or sparsely wooded, with coniferous forests at higher levels. Meltwater from the range feeds the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Amu Darya</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helmand-river">Helmand River</a> and the Kabul River, a major tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hindu Kush” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Chagataid armies also crossed the <b>Hindu Kush</b> to invade the Delhi Sultanate repeatedly from the 1290s, under commanders sent by Duwa such as his son Qutlugh Khwaja, who led a great army to the outskirts of Delhi in 1299.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chagatai-empire">Chagatai Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Ahmad then turned west to Mashhad, whose ruler Shahrukh Afshar acknowledged Afghan suzerainty, and sent an army north of the <b>Hindu Kush</b> that brought the Tajik, Hazara, Uzbek, Turkmen and other peoples of northern Afghanistan under his control.</blockquote><a href="/empire/durrani-empire">Durrani Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Alexander the Great crossed the <b>Hindu Kush</b> in 329 BCE and conquered Bactria, placing garrisons at Bactra, Maracanda and Cyropolis and settling Greek mercenaries in new colonies along the Oxus and the Jaxartes; he also massacred the Greek community settled there before him.</blockquote><a href="/empire/greco-bactrian-kingdom">Greco-Bactrian Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Their stronghold was Tokharistan, on the northern slopes of the <b>Hindu Kush</b> in today's southern Uzbekistan and northern Afghanistan, with a capital probably at Kunduz.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hephthalite-empire">Hephthalite Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The abundance of the coinage suggests large mining operations, particularly in the <b>Hindu Kush</b>, and an important monetary economy; the kings struck bilingual coins in both the Greek round standard and the Indian square standard, so money seems to have circulated through every part of society, and the…</blockquote><a href="/empire/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek Kingdom · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kushans were one of five branches of the Yuezhi, a people driven from the Gansu corridor by the Xiongnu in the 2nd century BCE, who settled in Bactria, north of the <b>Hindu Kush</b>, by about 130 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kushan-empire">Kushan Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
